Quite a number of them, as it happens. Most of them amount to glue code
(such as livecd-rootfs and casper, used for building and booting live
CDs) or tools necessary to build or install the distribution itself
(such as germinate, a tool for dependency analysis and expansion, or
ubiquity, the live CD installer, which was started elsewhere but most of
its development has happened in Ubuntu), but others that were written by
Ubuntu developers for Ubuntu include jockey-gtk, keymapper, readahead,
acpi-support, powermanagement-interface, ubufox, ubuntu-*, and various
others you could find if you chose to investigate.

It's more common for people or companies working on distributions to
contribute to existing packages instead, since of course the point of a
distribution is primarily to aggregate existing code originating
elsewhere; but that isn't to say that sometimes people working on
distributions don't identify a problem and write software from scratch
to address it.

(Note: I work for the company that founded Ubuntu - although I'm not
speaking for them here - hold significant copyright in a number of
packages in Ubuntu, am one of its core developers, and run the team that
decides whether software in its archive meets its licensing rules. You
can argue with me if you like but I do know a certain amount about the
subject. :-) )

