It seems that you are misremembering or misunderstanding section 3 of
GPLv2, or something similar. It says:

    b) Accompany it with a written offer, valid for at least three
    years, to give any third party, for a charge no more than your
    cost of physically performing source distribution, a complete
    machine-readable copy of the corresponding source code, to be
    distributed under the terms of Sections 1 and 2 above on a medium
    customarily used for software interchange; or,

(Note that this is one of three alternatives only one of which needs to
be followed.) Contrary to what you may have heard or believed, there are
no restrictions on what one is permitted to charge for binary
distributions; the GPL is instead concerned with ensuring that, once you
have a binary package, you can't be charged more than cost of materials
for the source code. It assumes that rather obvious market effects will
take care of people charging excessive sums for binary packages: that
is, nobody will buy from them because the next guy along is exercising
his right to give it away for free. Of course you might regard it as
immoral to attempt to charge, but that's not the same as the actual
restrictions imposed by the licence.

Like that of our parent distribution, Debian, the Ubuntu licensing
policy explicitly forbids the inclusion in Ubuntu's main components of
packages that restrict what you can charge for redistributing them as
components of an aggregate software distribution, and so David is quite
correct above. The major free software licences don't attempt to do this
so this doesn't generally turn out to be a problem.

Ubuntu is given away for free (largely, anyway; we do sell copies on
physical media such as DVDs and USB sticks for enough to cover costs)
not because the licences oblige us to, but because we believe charging
for software - particularly for free software! - is ultimately not a
viable model.


To keep this minimally on-topic, NetHack's licence (a variant of one
which eventually grew into the modern GPL) says:

    c) You may charge a distribution fee for the physical act of
    transferring a copy, and you may at your option offer warranty protection
    in exchange for a fee.

There's no limitation there on how large that fee is permitted to be.
The licence relies on the fact that people charging excessive sums are
essentially harmless because the next guy along can easily outcompete
them.

The current version, GPLv3, is more explicit, but it's still the same
basic idea:

    You may charge any price or no price for each copy that you convey,
  and you may offer support or warranty protection for a fee.

